"1. Am J Infect Control. 2025 Nov;53(11):1137-1143. doi:\n10.1016/j.ajic.2025.08.009.  Epub 2025 Aug 16.\n\nWorldwide antibiotic prescription practices in primary care and associated \nfactors: A systematic review and meta-analysis.\n\nSong Q(1), Li J(1), Zhou P(1), Chen R(1), Liu Z(2), Li H(3), Yin X(4).\n\nAuthor information:\n(1)Department of Social Medicine and Health Management, School of Public Health, \nTongji Medical College, Huazhong University of Science and Technology, Wuhan, \nHubei, China.\n(2)Shenzhen Guangming District People's Hospital, People's Republic of China, \nShenzhen, Guangdong, China.\n(3)School of Health Services Management, Anhui Medical University, Hefei, Anhui, \nChina. Electronic address: lihui2021@ahmu.edu.cn.\n(4)Department of Social Medicine and Health Management, School of Public Health, \nTongji Medical College, Huazhong University of Science and Technology, Wuhan, \nHubei, China. Electronic address: yxx@hust.edu.cn.\n\nBACKGROUND: Antibiotic prescription is a major driver of antimicrobial \nresistance. However, evidence on antibiotic prescription practices is inadequate \nglobally. This study aimed to assess antibiotic prescription practices and \nidentify its associated factors in primary care worldwide.\nMETHODS: PubMed, Embase, Web of science, and Global Index Medicus by WHO were \nsearched from studies published between January 2000 and September 2023. \nQuantitative studies that investigated the\u00a0antibiotic prescriptions practices \nwere included in this study. Each study was assessed by at least 2 independent \nreviewers. Risk of bias was assessed using a modified Hoy tool. Quantitative \nstudies were merged using Meta-analysis with random effects modeling. This study \nwas registered with PROSPERO, CRD42022354143.\nRESULTS: A total of 174 studies were included. The pooled prevalence of \nantibiotic prescribing was 42.1% (95%CI [39.2%, 45.1%]). Subgroup analysis \nindicated that antibiotic prescribing in primary care have not changed \nsignificantly over the past 20years. The prevalence of inappropriate antibiotics \nprescriptions was 57.6% (95%CI [43.4%, 71.2%]). Patients with higher education \nlevels were less likely to receive antibiotic prescriptions.\nCONCLUSIONS: We observed high prevalence of antibiotic prescribing in primary \ncare, especially in low- and middle-income countries. The prevalence of \nantibiotic prescription has not declined significantly over the past 20years. \nTherefore, it is necessary to review current antimicrobial stewardship \nstrategies.\n\nCopyright \u00a9 2025 Association for Professionals in Infection Control and \nEpidemiology, Inc. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.\n\nDOI: 10.1016/j.ajic.2025.08.009\nPMID: 40825457 [Indexed for MEDLINE]"